<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/685">685</a>–705</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan was an Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> who reigned from 685 to 705. He reunited the caliphate after a period of civil war, made Arabic the language of administration and introduced a distinctively Islamic gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coinage. He also commissioned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and his reforms gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> much of its lasting governmental structure.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abd-al-malik-ibn-marwan.jpg" alt="Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan" width="320" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_Umayyad_gold_dinar,_Caliph_Abd_al-Malik,_695_CE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Arab conquest of Egypt brought Islam in the 7th century, and at the turn of the 8th the Umayyad caliph <b>Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan</b> decreed that Arabic replace Greek as the sole language of administration.</blockquote><a href="/language/coptic">Coptic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In 688 the Byzantine emperor Justinian II and the Umayyad caliph <b>Abd al-Malik</b> agreed a treaty under which the revenues of Cyprus, along with those of Armenia and Iberia, were divided equally between them.</blockquote><a href="/state/arab-byzantine-condominium-of-cyprus">Arab-Byzantine Condominium of Cyprus</a></li><li><blockquote>The Umayyad victory at Marj Rahit in 684 kept Syria, Marwan I retook Egypt in 685, and <b>Abd al-Malik</b> reconquered Iraq, where Mus'ab was killed in 691.</blockquote><a href="/state/caliphate-of-ibn-al-zubayr">Caliphate of Ibn al-Zubayr</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Umayyads reunited the caliphate, an army sent by <b>Abd al-Malik</b> defeated Abu Fudayk in Bahrain in 693, ending the state.</blockquote><a href="/state/najdiyya">Najdiyya</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.47,39.61&amp;year=695" aria-label="Medina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5603 1263 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6053 1533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6053 1533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.47,39.61&amp;year=695">What was here in 695 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
